# Behavioural tests: continuation/thread/unwind runtime support.
#
# Ported from rt.x86.tst, which was Lua. These are the qc-- runtime's own
# regression tests for `cut to`, `foreign "C-- thread"`, and stack unwinding
# via the .pcmap data - exactly the paths docs/claude_notes/plan_tiger_hello.md
# flagged as "compiled but never proven at runtime", since hello.tig never
# calls into them.
#
# Unlike tiger.tests, none of these declare C-- globals, so they link
# against a runtime built with an EMPTY globals declaration (see run-rt.sh),
# not tests/build/tiger's - that one is baked for tiger's `bits32 alloc_ptr;`
# and its Cmm.globalsig hash would not match.
#
# Columns:  name  cmm-source  other-source  expected-rc  stdin ("-" for none)
# Expected stdout is always tests/cmm-pass/output/<name>.1

deadcontvar  deadcontvar.c--  deadcontvar-unwind.c  0  -
fact         fact.c--         factmain.c            0  -
fork         fork.c--         forkmain.c            0  -
nest         nest.c--         nestmain.c            0  -
trace        trace.c--        tracemain.c           0  -
unwind       unwind.c--       unwindt.c             0  -

# trace used to FAIL here: the stack walk stopped after 2 "Undescribed
# frame" entries instead of continuing through the rest of the C region back
# into C--. Root cause: it crosses C frames by chasing the %ebp chain (see
# runtime/gcc-linux.c's Cmm_c_change_activation), and tracemain.c called
# glibc's qsort - built without frame pointers on modern glibc, so the walk
# died one step into it. Fixed by swapping qsort for a hand-rolled sort
# tracemain.c compiles itself (see the "claude:" comment there) and passing
# -fno-omit-frame-pointer for cmm-pass/$other in run-rt.sh. The exact number of
# "Undescribed frame" lines in cmm-pass/output/trace.1 is an artifact of that
# sort's call depth, not a value worth matching exactly.
